  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the sum to $n$ terms of the AP $60+52+44+36+\\cdots$ is $S_n=4n(16-n)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$S_n=4n(16-n)$.",
          "worked": "$a=60$, $d=-8$.\n$$\\begin{align*}S_n&=\\frac n2[2a+(n-1)d]\\\\S_n&=\\frac n2[2(60)+(n-1)(-8)]\\\\S_n&=\\frac n2(120-8n+8)\\\\S_n&=\\frac n2(128-8n)\\\\S_n&=4n(16-n)\\end{align*}$$\nAs required.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q10-b",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence find how many terms must be taken to make the sum:",
        "layout": "grid",
        "columns": 2,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p25-q10-b-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "Zero",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "16 terms.",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}4n(16-n)&=0\\\\n&=0\\text{ or }n=16\\end{align*}$$\nSince $n>0$, take 16 terms.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 23
          },
          {
            "id": "p25-q10-b-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "Negative",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "More than 16 terms: $n\\ge17$.",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}4n(16-n)&<0\\\\n(16-n)&<0\\end{align*}$$\nSince $n>0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}16-n&<0\\\\n&>16\\end{align*}$$\nTake more than 16 terms.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 26
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find the two values of $n$ for which the sum $S_n$ is 220.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=5$ or $n=11$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}4n(16-n)&=220\\\\64n-4n^2&=220\\\\4n^2-64n+220&=0\\\\n^2-16n+55&=0\\\\(n-5)(n-11)&=0\\\\n&=5\\text{ or }n=11\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q10-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Show that $S_n=-144$ has two integer solutions, but that only one has meaning.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=18$ or $n=-2$; only $n=18$ has meaning because the number of terms must be positive.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}S_n&=-144\\\\4n(16-n)&=-144\\\\64n-4n^2&=-144\\\\4n^2-64n-144&=0\\\\n^2-16n-36&=0\\\\(n-18)(n+2)&=0\\\\n&=18\\text{ or }n=-2\\end{align*}$$\nOnly $n=18$ has meaning because $n$ must be positive.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q10-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "For what values of $n$ does the sum $S_n$ exceed 156?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=4,5,6,\\ldots,12$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}4n(16-n)&>156\\\\64n-4n^2&>156\\\\4n^2-64n+156&<0\\\\n^2-16n+39&<0\\\\(n-3)(n-13)&<0\\end{align*}$$\nSketching the upward-opening parabola with zeros at $3$ and $13$ shows that it is negative between the zeros.\n$$3<n<13$$\nSince $n$ is an integer, $n=4,5,6,\\ldots,12$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q10-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Prove that no sum $S_n$ can exceed 256.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$S_n=256-4(n-8)^2\\le256$, with equality when $n=8$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}S_n&=4n(16-n)\\\\S_n&=64n-4n^2\\end{align*}$$\nCompleting the square:\n$$\\begin{align*}S_n&=-4(n^2-16n)\\\\S_n&=-4(n-8)^2+256\\end{align*}$$\nThe parabola is concave down, with maximum value $256$ when $n=8$. Therefore no sum $S_n$ can exceed $256$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 31
      }
    ]
  },
